WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.230
So who's my guest this
week, the Data Scientist

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.943
and Facebook whistleblower,
Frances Haugen.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.160
Is the personal cost
outweighed by the wider good?

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.750
This is the whistleblower's dilemma.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.310
Frances Haugen calls her
decision to go public

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.285
with what she discovered
working at Facebook,

00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:21.320
not plan A, not even plan B, but plan J.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.140
But if it wasn't her initial intention,

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.550
it's now become central to her life.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.890
Sharing tens of thousands
of internal documents

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.837
with the media and US law enforcement.

00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:34.570
She wanted to show that Meta,
Facebook's parent company,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:38.540
knew the harm it was causing
and carried on regardless.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:41.440
The firm says Haugen's
claims paint a false picture,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.368
and it makes no commercial
sense to allow harmful content

00:00:44.368 --> 00:00:47.245
as advertisers don't want it.

00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:49.860
But you can only really whistle blow once.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.750
So what happens next?

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.200
And how do you keep up
the pressure for change

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.600
when you're now an outsider?

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.260
Here's what's coming up.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.760
Do you think of Facebook as
having blood on his hands?

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.070
Yes, unquestionably.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.760
Facebook's own documents say Instagram

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.640
isn't just bad for
teenagers, it's not just bad

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.710
for children, it's not just bad for them.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.100
It's actually worse than
other forms of social media,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.090
because TikTok is about performance.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.000
It's about humor.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.970
It's about doing things with your friends.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.320
Snapchat is about faces,
about augmented reality.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.160
Reddit is at least nominally
about ideas, right?

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.293
But Instagram is about
bodies and social comparison.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.950
Frances, thank you so much for being here

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.750
for talking to me today and to all of us.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:46.583
Thank you for inviting me.

00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:47.416
Happy to be here.

00:01:47.416 --> 00:01:50.680
Well, I mean, your voice
has become very famous.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.490
Indeed, I wanted to start by
asking how it's been since

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.234
you've gone public as a whistleblower.

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:57.972
You know, it's so interesting.

00:01:57.972 --> 00:02:02.944
I live in Puerto Rico and no
one ever recognizes me there.

00:02:02.944 --> 00:02:06.260
And I've been, I've been
really shocked at like,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.200
in some ways my life has
changed a lot, right?

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.060
Like I can sleep now at night.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.363
So that's always a good thing.

00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:14.560
I do interviews like this,
which is still surreal to me,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.773
but you know,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.650
it's enough separated from,
like, San Francisco that,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.650
you know, I'm never
recognized in that context.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.030
And so my life hasn't
really changed that much

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.650
on a day to day basis.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.878
I'm amazed that I can
have my DMs open online.

00:02:27.878 --> 00:02:30.440
Like you can DM me on Twitter and like,

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.890
I don't even get harassed.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.460
And so I know that compared
to like female politicians,

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.250
I've had an easy experience.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.130
Has that changed?

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.050
Was it different when
you first went public?

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.850
Oh, no and even when I first went public.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:42.683
Okay.

00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:46.134
I've gotten like maybe,
maybe 10 mean messages ever.

00:02:46.134 --> 00:02:49.300
And like, I always respond to them, so.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.630
No one troll me.
What's your technique?

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.310
I don't know, I'm always
willing to talk to my critics.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.970
And how, do they usually calm
down a bit at that point?

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.734
They usually don't respond, you know.

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:00.974
Who'd have thought they'd be rude, hey,

00:03:00.974 --> 00:03:02.310
but that's interesting.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.427
You also say now you can sleep at night.

00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:05.304
Yes.

00:03:05.304 --> 00:03:06.880
Were you not sleeping, when
you worked at Facebook?

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.970
It's really hard living
with a secret, you know,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.480
like I think one of the
things I had never really

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.310
contemplated before I came
out was whistleblowers,

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:16.640
in general, are not in very good shape

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.190
by the time they come out.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.660
And like, one of the things that I had

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.430
that I think was very
different about my experience

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.240
versus most people's is I lived

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.720
with my parents during COVID.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.715
And so I had people who
I completely trusted

00:03:26.715 --> 00:03:29.216
who I could talk to things about.

00:03:29.216 --> 00:03:31.750
And so by the time like I came
out or by the time I acted,

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.200
like I was already aligned
with like what I needed to do.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.980
And I think for a lot of
people who are whistleblowers,

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.720
they live alone with a secret
where they know people's lives

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.530
are on the line and that
process really eats away at you.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.930
And, you know, I know
that I did what I could.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.910
And so there's a wonderful
release in that, right.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.430
It's very easy to sleep at night then.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.080
But I suppose with that, I mean,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.700
and you were not sleeping.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.270
You were stressed about what you knew.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:00.930
We'll get into that.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.250
Did you need a certain
level of grit, resilience

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.130
to actually go through
with it would you say

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.440
because of course, you know,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.210
there are different types of whistleblower

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.930
in different circumstances.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.430
Oh, unquestionably, we, actually I think,

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.630
as a society need to talk
more about the continuum

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.510
of whistle blowing, like in
the United States, you know,

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.230
you can't sign a contract
that prevents you from giving

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.520
information to Congress that
you believe is necessary

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.173
for Congress to do oversight.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.720
You can be a very subtle,
quiet whistleblower.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.700
It's just that people see
the few whistleblowers

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:30.960
that are out.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.970
And so I think it's one of
these things where if you know

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.193
that what you need to do is necessary.

00:04:35.193 --> 00:04:37.879
You know, I've always been
a long distance cyclist,

00:04:37.879 --> 00:04:40.067
you know, you just put
on more miles, you know,

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:44.030
you just keep going and I'm really happy

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.270
with how things ended up.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.644
Were you scared just before, you know,

00:04:47.644 --> 00:04:48.520
what was that moment like?

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.436
Yeah.

00:04:49.436 --> 00:04:50.440
I dunno even know what the moment was.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.273
Yeah.

00:04:51.273 --> 00:04:52.970
That you blew the whistle.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.017
So I think there's like
different sets of moments.

00:04:55.017 --> 00:04:58.770
So, while I was getting the
information out of Facebook,

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.440
I was very worried that I wouldn't be able

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.460
to get out the information
that was needed.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.150
Right, that I knew that
this was very complicated

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:11.490
that if you didn't get a
representative set of documents

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.620
that, you know, you could criticize it

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.400
and say, this is not a full picture.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.130
And so, until I was done,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.950
I was very nervous that
I would not finish,

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.820
but I never worried about
consequences because like,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.330
the number of lives that are on the line

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.500
is astronomical, right?

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.160
Like, I earnestly believe
there are 10 or 20 million

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.480
lives on the line in some
of the most fragile places

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.313
in the world.

00:05:34.313 --> 00:05:36.550
When you say on the
line, what do you mean?

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.950
So so far we've had two major
ethnic violence instances,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.037
genocides, that were, they were
facilitated by social media.

00:05:43.037 --> 00:05:46.910
So, we had one in Myanmar
where over 200,000 people died.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:48.250
We've had another one in Ethiopia

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:49.940
over the last couple of years

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.230
and we should not expect
that to be the end.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.250
We should expect that to be the beginning.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.300
And, but what's the difference
between using social media

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.300
to organize and using,
I don't know, email.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.190
Oh, I'm so glad you asked.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.870
That's a wonderful question.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.060
So in email, the service
provider in general

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.310
is not telling you what order
to read your email in, right?

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.970
There are not algorithms
that give distribution

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.610
to some ideas and not to others.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.770
In the case of Facebook,
the algorithm changes

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.300
they made in 2018 to go
from just trying to keep you

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.970
on the site as long as
possible to trying to optimize

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:25.942
for eliciting a reaction from you,

00:06:25.942 --> 00:06:29.260
changed what content got amplified.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.130
So, when they made that change,

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:34.180
political parties across Europe,
on the left, on the right

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.527
said, "Hey, we know you
changed the algorithm

00:06:37.527 --> 00:06:40.197
"because it used to be we could
share the bread and butter

00:06:40.197 --> 00:06:41.947
"of democracy, like a white paper

00:06:41.947 --> 00:06:43.847
"on our agricultural policy

00:06:43.847 --> 00:06:45.337
"and it didn't get the most comments,

00:06:45.337 --> 00:06:47.717
"but we could see in the
statistics it got read.

00:06:47.717 --> 00:06:49.747
"And now if we share
that exact same content,

00:06:49.747 --> 00:06:51.320
it's just crickets."

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.410
And it's because the algorithm now says,

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:57.180
unless we can provoke a reaction
from you, it's not good.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.340
And when you combine that
bias in the algorithm,

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.180
that extreme content gets
the most distribution

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.690
with linguistically diverse places

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:07.860
like the most fragile places on earth

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.510
are often linguistically diverse.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.453
That means they're using

00:07:11.453 --> 00:07:13.050
the most dangerous version of Facebook.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.160
You know, no safety systems
'cause Facebook doesn't think

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.330
there's enough people
that speak that language

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.600
and societies are already fragile.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.420
And so it's just very dangerous.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.950
And we've seen, more and more times where

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.080
that gets weaponized in a
context that leads to tragedy.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.530
I mean, it's safe to
say with this assertion

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:30.363
and other assertions,

00:07:30.363 --> 00:07:32.620
Facebook does not agree
with your view of it.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:33.453
I'm sure.

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:34.570
It says that, you know, it, you know,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.010
the idea of promoting hate,

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.660
that's just not what advertisers want.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.570
So why would they do that?

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:39.670
It doesn't make sense for business

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:40.690
and that they don't do that.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:43.000
And also with some of the issues,

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.090
they recognize them and
they're working on them.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.160
So what's interesting is we
really need to understand

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.090
that Facebook is not a single product,

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.530
that Facebook in different
parts of the world

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.440
are very different experiences.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.520
Like when there's a multiple
hundred page report from the UN

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.650
that talks about how Facebook
was used by the junta

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.410
in Myanmar to inflame the genocide.

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.760
Like this is not a matter of contention.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.388
Like it's been very well documented.

00:08:07.388 --> 00:08:11.530
Facebook spun up war rooms
in the lead up to Ethiopia

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:14.870
'cause they knew the roles
that they were contributing to

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.572
and they knew that was a risk factor.

00:08:16.572 --> 00:08:20.286
And yet they still see
themselves, not as a publisher.

00:08:20.286 --> 00:08:21.388
Yes.

00:08:21.388 --> 00:08:22.390
As a platform.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:23.463
You've sat there while someone

00:08:23.463 --> 00:08:27.140
from the Facebook oversight
board, talking to me about that.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.170
Helle Thorning-Schmidt.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.230
Yeah.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:30.730
I mean, I don't know
if you think that board

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.570
is fit for purpose, they said
they were very interested

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.040
to meet and talk with you.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.600
We've met with them.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.140
Do you think it's fit for purpose?

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.730
So the oversight board is
a beautiful illustration

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.700
of how effective Facebook's
communications department is

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.440
because most people hear a name

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.070
like Facebook Oversight
Board and you're like, oh,

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:48.990
there's an independent
body that can ask questions

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:49.860
about Facebook.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.020
They could ask is Facebook
inflaming violence

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.280
with algorithms.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.500
Turns out that's not even in their remit.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:00.180
That when you look at what
they're actually responsible for,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.430
Facebook spent 120 million
dollars endowing a board

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.000
that can only look at
content censorship decisions.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.250
So it's not fit for purpose.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.520
It's not fit for purpose.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:09.810
And we need an oversight board

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.300
that actually can do
oversight on the company

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.400
or we need something like
the Digital Services Act.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.240
Which of course, you know,
countries around the world

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.710
are looking at different
ways at this at the moment.

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:21.070
I know you've been contributing to that.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.370
Do you think you've done anything

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.503
that's made any changes though?

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:27.380
What points towards that?

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:30.470
So, Facebook has tried to expand coverage

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.070
of its language classifiers.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.640
So in the Fall they rolled
out a different way of trying

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.930
to run their censorship system
so they could cover more

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:40.210
languages, which was one
of my core criticisms

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:42.650
was that not enough
safety systems existed.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.920
They were misleading the public
about which safety systems

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.770
existed in which languages.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.590
Facebook could have
released parental controls

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:52.630
for Instagram at any point
in the last 10 years.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:55.530
People have been begging for
even basic parental controls

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:56.860
for 10 years.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.480
They rolled out parental
controls for the first time,

00:09:58.480 --> 00:09:59.540
maybe two months ago.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.620
And you directly think
that's linked to what you-

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:03.453
I think so.

00:10:03.453 --> 00:10:04.460
Took with you and showed?

00:10:06.086 --> 00:10:08.830
A number of major leaders on
the Children's Rights Bill

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.100
in the United States have
said that the reason why their

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.610
bill is moving forward is
because of my disclosures.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.370
And I think pressure
like that is part of why

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:16.630
they have parental controls today.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:18.660
I mean, because let's talk about,

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.560
with particular reference,
Facebook, of course,

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.970
the parent owner of Instagram.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.610
I mean, what would you say
to those individuals who talk

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.750
about the role for better parenting,

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.640
the role for autonomy,

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.120
and looking after young people online,

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.550
and that not necessarily
the platform should

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.510
be held responsible.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.640
So, I'll give you an example
of how it works today.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.295
And I'll give you an example
of something that's a really

00:10:42.295 --> 00:10:44.790
low hanging fruit that they
could do that would put control

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:46.040
in the hands of kids, put control

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.590
in the hands of parents.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.670
Today, Facebook has run studies

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:52.210
where they take a brand
new account, blank account

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.330
and they search for some
pretty innocuous stuff

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:56.330
like healthy eating, healthy recipes.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.590
'Cause to be honest, we
could all eat healthier

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.740
and all they do is they
click on the first five

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.690
or 10 posts each day and
they follow any hashtags

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:04.940
they get suggested.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.000
So there's no friends,
there's no other interest,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.300
they're just clicking on
it, it's just the algorithm.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.920
Within two or three weeks, you get sent

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:14.210
to pro-anorexia content
and pro-self harm content.

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.000
So, that's how the algorithm works today.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.643
They're pushing kids towards
more extreme content.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.510
What could be happening is
Facebook could start asking kids,

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.747
they already take all
the content on Instagram

00:11:24.747 --> 00:11:26.240
and make into clusters.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.700
So there's maybe 15,000 topics,
a hundred thousand topics.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.120
They could go and poll kids,
they do things like this

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:35.710
already and say, does this
make your body image worse?

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.830
Does this make your make
you feel bad about yourself?

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:42.360
And if they saw a child who
was consuming a thousand,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.250
2,000 pieces of content a
day that were about topics

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.410
that make kids depressed or
make them have worse body image,

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:49.800
they could ask that kid,

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.702
do you wanna keep looking at this content?

00:11:51.702 --> 00:11:53.870
Or do you wanna see less of this content?

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.398
Because children are saying today,

00:11:55.398 --> 00:11:57.160
I'm working with this therapist.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:58.670
I'm trying to make better decisions.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:01.970
And the content follows
me around Instagram.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:06.060
Like I have to give up
my friends to be safe.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:08.200
And I think that's like too high a bar.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.220
But if my child was watching
a thousand bits of content,

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.950
2000 bits of content, that
takes quite a lot of time.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.330
I would hope I knew
about that in some way.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:17.560
That might sound very naive.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:18.860
My child's pretty young at the moment,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.880
but there is also that role there,

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.210
I suppose that some worry
that by putting it all

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.550
on the social media company,

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:28.370
without perhaps even going
against what you're saying here.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:29.203
Yeah.

00:12:29.203 --> 00:12:31.520
That that role is being
downplayed or ignored.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.030
So I think one way to look
at is I think good parenting

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.400
plays like a vital role, but
one of the things we need

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.470
to keep in mind and I
lived with this experience

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.040
when I was a teenager,

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.080
you can feel confident
about your parenting,

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.372
but you need to ask the question,

00:12:43.372 --> 00:12:46.360
are the parents of every
one of your kids' friends,

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:48.780
are they being as
attentive or is every kid,

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.480
is every parent in your kid's
school being that attentive.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:52.900
But I think it's an uphill fight.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.930
'Cause these products are
designed to be sticky,

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.560
to be addictive 'cause they're
more profitable that way.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.300
I suppose there's a difference
between knowing something

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.260
is bad and keeping going
with it because it's good

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:07.000
for business and thinking
perhaps there isn't

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:10.144
or aren't that many things
you can do to change it.

00:13:10.144 --> 00:13:12.817
Which is it with Mark Zuckerberg?

00:13:12.817 --> 00:13:15.043
I think it's a question of,

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.850
I think Mark struggles with
so like I think he genuinely

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.760
believes that Facebook
is just a mirror, right?

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.010
That, you know, there's always
been bad things in the world.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:25.270
There've always been kids
with eating disorders.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:26.730
There've always been kids
who have been bullied.

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:29.140
You know, this is just a
mirror and you're unhappy

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:32.860
because you can see it now and
he has trouble acknowledging

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:35.570
his power or the power of these products.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:37.470
But you are saying he
just sees it as a mirror

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:40.070
rather than he knows it's doing harm.

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:42.250
I think it's the thing that
he tells himself a story

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.729
that he's surrounded by advisors.

00:13:43.729 --> 00:13:45.170
And they're not telling him either.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:46.440
And they're saying no, no, no.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.140
The world has always had bad things.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:49.650
Like you it's just a mirror.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.090
It sounds like a cult.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:55.180
It is a little, it definitely
has some management issues.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:56.210
If you were Mark Zuckerberg,

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.553
do you think you should
be able to sleep at night?

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.510
I think he's really stressed.

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.220
Right, like I think the fact
that he has this vision,

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:05.250
that we're all gonna
live in the metaverse.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:06.680
Right, think about that a little bit,

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.180
like the metaverse was a dystopian concept

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.820
in the original novel, right?

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.470
Like people's lives were so bad

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:15.000
that they went into these virtual spaces.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.480
I bet it's really hard for
Mark to go to a restaurant.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:19.230
Like, can you imagine
people like looking over

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.860
and glaring at you?

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:21.720
Do you think that they do though?

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:22.690
I think they do.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:25.490
Like in, in Silicon Valley,
people are not pro Facebook.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:27.760
I mean, you sound like
you're sympathetic to him

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.550
and yet at the same
time you couldn't sleep

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:30.990
because of what you knew

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:32.710
and you didn't obviously know everything.

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.740
I think hurt people hurt people.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.640
And I think Mark has
been surrounded by people

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.280
since he was 19 years old who told him

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.150
he was doing a great job.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:41.450
And so it's one of these things of,

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.400
we can demonize Mark Zuckerberg,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.540
but it's not gonna make him heal faster.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.009
It's not gonna like when
people feel attacked.

00:14:48.009 --> 00:14:48.842
But he has the power.

00:14:48.842 --> 00:14:49.749
He does have the power.

00:14:49.749 --> 00:14:50.626
So that's not the issue.

00:14:50.626 --> 00:14:51.459
He's the only one who has the power

00:14:51.459 --> 00:14:53.410
because the voting structure
of Facebook is different

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:57.090
than most corporations in
that he holds 56% of the votes

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:57.923
for the company.

00:14:57.923 --> 00:15:01.070
No one, but Mark Zuckerberg
can control Facebook right now.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:02.240
Do you think he should go?

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.610
I don't think he, I don't
think the company can recover

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.280
as long as he is the leader of it.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.640
And I think the fact that he
doubled down on the Metaverse

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.760
when I brought up issues around genocide,

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.470
like building, saying
we have enough people,

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.910
we have enough money, enough
people to hire 10,000 people

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.580
to build video games

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:22.380
instead of actually
making these systems safe.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:24.090
I think that's a dereliction of duty.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.620
I mean, the company is at pains to mention

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:30.140
40,000 moderators and that, you know,

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:32.780
they're spending a lot more on this,

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:33.977
giving more attention to it.

00:15:33.977 --> 00:15:36.030
But of course, as you say,
you mentioned the Metaverse,

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:38.190
there's 10 billion, I
believe to be spent on that.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:39.410
So, when my disclosures happened,

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:41.860
they increased the spending
from about two and a half,

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:44.861
2.6 billion per year to 5
billion a year, which is great.

00:15:44.861 --> 00:15:46.410
On moderation?

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:47.243
On safety systems.

00:15:47.243 --> 00:15:48.220
Safety, okay.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:50.030
And great, that's a step
in the right direction,

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:53.267
but did you know they bought
back 75 billion dollars

00:15:53.267 --> 00:15:56.660
of stock in the 12 month period
that's happening right now

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.720
so that they have enough
money lying around

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.480
to light on fire $75 billion,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.640
but they could only spend
$5 billion on safety.

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:06.930
They have $10 billion
to spend on video games,

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.110
but they can't spend more on safety.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:10.920
You talked about, of
course, what's been going on

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:13.870
in different countries and
the concerns that you had

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:15.420
about lives on the line.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.476
But there have also been some
cases I'm not gonna name names

00:16:18.476 --> 00:16:21.160
for reasons that you know, are obvious,

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:22.330
we're talking about children here,

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:25.800
but there have been examples
of children in both the US,

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.020
the UK, and I'm sure
elsewhere in the world

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.050
where they have taken their own lives

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:34.790
and the parents blame, you
know, Facebook, this company,

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.550
you know, as part of that Instagram,

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:38.550
they also blame some other
social media platforms,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:39.670
I should say.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.820
But do you think of with
that particular instance

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.110
with regards to young people,

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.789
do you think of Facebook as
having blood on its hands?

00:16:45.789 --> 00:16:47.900
Yes, unquestionably.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.570
Facebook's own documents say Instagram

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:53.150
isn't just bad for
teenagers, it's not just bad

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:54.840
for children 'cause remember there's kids

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:56.300
all the way down to eight and nine,

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:59.790
like parents have told me,
kids are using social media,

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:03.331
using voice to text as
young as eight and nine.

00:17:03.331 --> 00:17:07.470
That it's not just bad for
them, it's actually worse

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:09.050
than other forms of social media

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:11.090
because TikTok is about performance.

00:17:11.090 --> 00:17:11.980
It's about humor.

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.980
It's about doing things with your friends.

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:17.290
Snapchat is about faces,
about augmented reality.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:21.008
Reddit is at least nominally
about ideas, right?

00:17:21.008 --> 00:17:25.000
But Instagram is about
bodies and social comparison.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.350
So you do see when those parents have said

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.780
what they have said about their children.

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:32.160
You buy what they're saying
about the role of those

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.290
companies, this company in particular.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.610
Unquestionably, like the one
they broke my heart the most

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:38.850
was there was a father
of a child who died,

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:39.850
who I got to meet with.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.537
And he said, "I don't know what I

00:17:41.537 --> 00:17:42.467
"could have done differently.

00:17:42.467 --> 00:17:43.987
"I took his phone away.

00:17:43.987 --> 00:17:45.747
"Like, I saw that he was
looking at this content.

00:17:45.747 --> 00:17:47.576
"I saw he was getting bullied.

00:17:47.576 --> 00:17:49.597
"I took his phone away
and he ran away from home.

00:17:49.597 --> 00:17:52.327
"And when we looked for him,
we couldn't find him anywhere.

00:17:52.327 --> 00:17:53.597
"It wasn't any of his friend's houses.

00:17:53.597 --> 00:17:55.917
"And the reason was cause he
fell asleep on a park bench

00:17:55.917 --> 00:17:57.467
"and he had hypothermia.

00:17:57.467 --> 00:17:59.168
"And so I gave him his phone back.

00:17:59.168 --> 00:18:02.220
Like I don't know what I
could have done differently."

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.470
And that's what parents are facing.

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:06.010
They feel powerless because
these systems are designed

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.160
to be sticky.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:08.750
They're not designed for children.

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:10.360
They're designed for adults.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.980
It might seem a slightly old question.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.670
Would you ever go back
there and try and improve it

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:15.540
and work there?

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.330
I still maintain, I still
encourage people to work

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:19.700
at Facebook because it is the most,

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:22.190
we have to solve the problem of Facebook.

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:24.583
But you weren't sleeping
when you worked at Facebook.

00:18:24.583 --> 00:18:26.632
I mean, I wouldn't feel
like it sounds like a good

00:18:26.632 --> 00:18:29.281
job move for me if we were having a chat.

00:18:29.281 --> 00:18:31.740
I'm not having to hold those
lies in anymore, right?

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:35.270
But I suppose if you're
saying to people go,

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:37.730
you couldn't make the
change while you were there.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.150
So, the way, the thing I
always say to people is

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:41.896
these systems are very complicated.

00:18:41.896 --> 00:18:45.170
There are currently zero
classes you can take anywhere

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:47.560
in the world where you will
learn how Facebook's algorithms

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:51.240
work or the interconnection
of how the algorithms work

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:52.560
with the product choices.

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:55.220
And we need good people
to go into these companies

00:18:56.214 --> 00:18:58.370
and participate with their eyes open

00:18:58.370 --> 00:18:59.490
because the way the world learns.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:00.920
It sounds like they're going to

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:02.427
'cause they're not gonna sleep, yeah.

00:19:02.427 --> 00:19:06.330
I believe we will only
get better social media

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.800
if we engage in the process of
building better social media.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.330
So if you were given the call, you would?

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:12.549
I would go back.

00:19:12.549 --> 00:19:13.620
Had the call?

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:14.500
I've never had the call.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.670
I, they don't really acknowledge I exist.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:18.504
They'll definitely know your name.

00:19:18.504 --> 00:19:21.850
Just looking across the
social media landscape,

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:24.150
what do you make of it may not happen,

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.740
but there's somebody like Elon Musk trying

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.730
to take over Twitter and grapple
with the free speech issue.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:33.000
He's also talked about
reinstating, for instance,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:34.640
Donald Trump's account.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:35.757
What do you make of that?

00:19:35.757 --> 00:19:38.240
And what he's said so
far about free speech,

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.930
there's some who have
expressed concerns about him,

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:43.870
Elon Musk, as a person having this power.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.590
You know, I think it's so
hard to read Elon's actions

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:51.590
because he does so many,
he, at this, at one point

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:55.400
at the same time, he is able
to land rockets on the moon.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:56.410
Upright, yep.

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:58.617
Upright, land them up and
down, all those things.

00:19:58.617 --> 00:20:01.790
So, he must be good at
asking hard questions

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:03.120
and accepting hard answers.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.210
'Cause if you're in denial on
the level of Mark Zuckerberg,

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:06.470
there's no way you're going to the moon.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:07.890
Okay.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:11.420
But he also does all these
wacky things on the internet.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:14.680
And so it's this question of
like, which is the real Elon?

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.340
And so part of the reason why
I'm cautiously optimistic,

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.980
I don't know, is I think once
he gets inside the company

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:23.260
and he can see all the data
and he can talk to the people

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:26.780
and get a more robust picture,
I would hope that he would,

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:28.560
you know, there's a lot of the
things that I'm recommending

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:31.880
are around, looking at
behavior over content.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:34.170
Because when we look at
things like saying, you know,

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:36.530
should you have to click
a link to reshare it?

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:39.310
How long should you have
to have that link open?

00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:40.740
Clicking on a link before you reshare it

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.840
reduces misinformation by 10 or 15%.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.930
These are interventions
that work in every language

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:47.200
in the world.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.500
And so I'm cautiously optimistic

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.550
based on who his advisors are,

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:53.252
that there would be an opportunity

00:20:53.252 --> 00:20:55.027
to do many of these things.

00:20:55.027 --> 00:20:55.860
And part of why they
haven't been done to date

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.500
is they cut off little slices of profit.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:01.330
And so, I think there's
opportunities to explore solutions

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:03.550
that weren't possible when
it was a public company.

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:05.680
But you know, time will tell.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.270
Have you made friends with
other whistle blowers?

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:08.103
Do you know some?

00:21:08.103 --> 00:21:09.110
I do know some other whistleblowers.

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:10.730
Is there a community of you?

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:12.690
They are very friendly.

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:14.525
I wouldn't say that we have that we have,

00:21:14.525 --> 00:21:17.320
there might be an active
community, but I've talked

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:20.100
to a handful of them on an ongoing way.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.210
I wonder if there's a kind of, you know,

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:23.560
sometimes when you take a leap.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:24.393
Yeah.

00:21:24.393 --> 00:21:26.060
Someone else who's taken a
leap before kind of steps

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:27.730
in and says, I know what
you're going through.

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:29.617
And you might wanna think
about this, this and this.

00:21:29.617 --> 00:21:33.760
What's so interesting is
there's one whistleblower

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.770
who I've talked to, who has really,

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:37.280
who took a very different path than me.

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:39.840
And they went it very alone, right?

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.980
Like they, they have never accepted

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.580
any kind of support or assistance.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:46.776
And like I very early on got,

00:21:46.776 --> 00:21:49.300
or started working with
a whistle blowing lawyer

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:52.693
and they, whistleblower aid
throughout the United States.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:57.700
And they were incredibly
beneficial in terms of educating me

00:21:57.700 --> 00:22:00.340
on like, you think you
have made some choices,

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:01.690
but let me tell you about the consequences

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:02.850
of those choices, right?

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:05.860
Like, I had always intended
not to come out, right?

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.400
Like, that's part of why
the disclosures are so large

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.290
is that I wanted them
to stand on their own.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:14.170
And because they were able
to take time and like talk me

00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:16.990
through consequences, they
were like, your identity

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.940
is gonna be the juiciest
story in the world.

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:21.530
Like, and Facebook knows who you are.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.700
Like they know who pulled
the doc, like which documents

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.533
and whatever.

00:22:24.533 --> 00:22:25.820
I'm sure they know who you are.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:28.940
And so I talked to this other person

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.528
who had not gotten the
same amount of handholding.

00:22:31.528 --> 00:22:33.700
And it's just so interesting.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:35.400
You know, every
whistleblower is different.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.580
And the thing I would encourage
whistleblowers is like,

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:38.930
always ask for help.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:40.420
People wanna help you.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.168
And you don't have to be public.

00:22:42.168 --> 00:22:44.070
I did wanna ask how you relax

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:47.370
when you're not blowing
whistles and keeping data

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.890
and then sharing it and
trying to tell the world

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:51.670
what you are concerned about,
what's keeping you awake.

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:52.900
You mentioned cycling.

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.170
Is that the main or what
else do you like to do?

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:56.720
I love the ocean.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:59.530
And so I have not, I
don't have my bike set up,

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:03.400
but the ocean is beautiful
and we have snorkel equipment

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.083
and there's sea turtles.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:08.224
Is that not the most wonderful thing?

00:23:08.224 --> 00:23:09.057
And you can just lay there in the water

00:23:09.057 --> 00:23:10.880
and like follow a sea
turtle and you're like,

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.300
nothing else matters.

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:13.950
So I'm very lucky.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:15.460
That's a beautiful image to paint.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:16.370
I mean I'm sure you've shared

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:18.082
some photos of it on Instagram.

00:23:18.082 --> 00:23:20.980
My Instagram's only like the pictures

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:22.223
of me meeting people.

00:23:23.141 --> 00:23:25.770
I'm not very good at, ironically,

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.340
I'm not very good at Instagram, so.

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:28.868
There you go.

00:23:28.868 --> 00:23:29.730
That's the scoop.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:31.020
Frances, thank you very
much for talking to me.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.671
My pleasure, good to be here

00:23:32.671 --> 00:23:34.990
It's been really fascinating
and thank you so much

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:36.180
for being with us.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:38.973
Until we meet again, do
take care and goodbye.

